<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for The Curator.

  ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
  ║  INERT TODAY. Entitlements are applied at CODE-SIGNING time, and this     ║
  ║  build is deliberately unsigned (`mac.identity: null`). This file exists  ║
  ║  so the reasoning is on record BEFORE the first signed build, not after   ║
  ║  someone spends an afternoon on a picker that silently does nothing.      ║
  ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime turns
  several things that work fine today into hard failures. The four below are
  the ones this app actually needs.

  ── The three Electron ones ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  V8 compiles JavaScript at runtime. Under the hardened runtime that is a
  code-injection violation unless allow-jit is granted, and Electron's own
  documentation pairs it with allow-unsigned-executable-memory. The dyld one
  is needed because Electron's helper processes are launched with
  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE and friends in the environment.

  ── The AppleEvents one, and the honest uncertainty about it ────────────────
  INFERRED, NOT MEASURED — nothing here has been built, so this could not be
  tested. State of the reasoning:

    `src/routes/config.js`'s POST /api/config/pick-folder — the folder picker
    an EXISTING USER hits on their very first screen, to point the app at a
    knowledge folder they already have — shells out to:

        osascript -e 'POSIX path of (choose folder with prompt "…")'

    `choose folder` is a StandardAdditions command, and my reading is that it
    is handled IN the osascript process rather than sent as an Apple Event to
    another application. On that reading, com.apple.security.automation.apple-
    events is NOT strictly required. I am not confident enough in that to bet
    the first-run experience on it, and the asymmetry is not close: granting
    the entitlement costs one key and one usage string, while being wrong
    about it costs the single most important action an existing user takes.

    IT IS GRANTED HERE DEFENSIVELY. That is a hedge, and it is labelled as
    one. It is not a substitute for measuring it on a real signed build.

  ── The sharper hazard, which this file CANNOT fix ──────────────────────────
  Whatever grants the picker, the app then READS the chosen folder. If that
  folder is under ~/Documents, ~/Desktop or ~/Downloads — where a "second
  brain" folder very plausibly lives — macOS TCC gates it, and a hardened,
  notarized app with no usage-description string in Info.plist is KILLED
  rather than gracefully denied. The NS*FolderUsageDescription strings are set
  in electron-builder.yml's `mac.extendInfo` for that reason; they are a
  DIFFERENT mechanism from the entitlements in this file and neither covers
  the other.

  ── And the failure would not even look like a failure ──────────────────────
  Read the picker route's catch block: it treats `err.code === 1` as "the user
  pressed Cancel" and answers `{cancelled: true}`. `osascript` exits 1 on a
  script error too — including a permission refusal. So a TCC or entitlement
  denial is not merely silent, it is MISREPORTED to the frontend as a
  deliberate user cancellation, which is the hardest possible thing to
  diagnose from a support conversation. That is a defect in `src/`, outside
  this change's scope, and it is reported rather than patched.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
